<!-- ─── PAGE: JOB TYPES ─── -->
<div id="pg-jobtypes" class="page">
  <div class="hero">
    <div class="part-badge">Part A · Section 2</div>
    <h2>Job Types — Permanent, Contract &amp; Contract-to-Hire</h2>
    <p>Every job order falls into one of three categories. Your entire pitch to the candidate changes based on which one it is.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="content-wrap">

    <h2 class="sec-title">The Three Job Types</h2>
    <p class="sec-sub">Learn the definition, what the candidate receives, and the exact language to use on a call.</p>

    <div class="cards-grid">
      <div class="card perm">
        <div class="card-badge">PERM — Permanent / Direct Hire</div>
        <h3>Full-Time Employee</h3>
        <p>Candidate is hired directly by the client company as a full-time employee. No defined end date. The staffing agency earns a one-time placement fee (typically 15–25% of first-year salary).</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Full salary with annual increases</li>
          <li>Health, dental, vision, 401k, PTO</li>
          <li>Job security and legal employment protections</li>
          <li>Career development and promotion path</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="pitch">This is a permanent, full-time role with the company itself. You would be their direct employee with full benefits from day one.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card contract">
        <div class="card-badge">C — Contract (Staff Aug)</div>
        <h3>Defined-Duration Contract</h3>
        <p>Candidate works on a defined-duration contract — typically 3–13 weeks (healthcare) or 6–12 months (IT). The agency is the employer of record. Client pays a bill rate per hour.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Hourly rate (higher than equivalent perm salary)</li>
          <li>W-2 via agency (benefits limited or none)</li>
          <li>Defined end date — must find next role when contract ends</li>
          <li>No guarantee of extension or conversion</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="pitch">This is a 6-month contract through our agency. Your pay is $XX/hr on W-2. Many of these roles extend — but I can't promise that upfront.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="card c2h">
        <div class="card-badge">C2H — Contract-to-Hire</div>
        <h3>Trial Period → Permanent</h3>
        <p>Starts as a contract (typically 3–6 months) with a built-in possibility of converting to permanent employment if both parties agree. Very common in IT staffing.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Higher hourly rate during contract phase</li>
          <li>Trial period to assess the company</li>
          <li>Clear path to permanent role if it goes well</li>
          <li>Bridged to full benefits upon conversion</li>
        </ul>
        <div class="pitch">This starts as a 6-month contract at $XX/hr, but the client wants to convert the right person to permanent. Think of it as a paid interview.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout info">
      <div class="callout-icon">💡</div>
      <div class="callout-body">
        <h4>The "Paid Interview" Framing for C2H</h4>
        <p>Candidates who push back on C2H with "But I want a permanent job" are most effectively addressed with the paid interview reframe. It acknowledges their goal (permanent employment) while reframing the risk (contract period) as a benefit (trial period). Use this language every time.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <hr class="divider">
    <h2 class="sec-title">The C2H Rate-to-Salary Conversion Rule</h2>
    <p class="sec-sub">Candidates often ask: "Why is the contract rate only $65/hr? The equivalent perm salary would be $90k." Here is how to answer that accurately.</p>

    <div class="formula">
      <span class="comment">// The 2,000-hour rule</span><br>
      Hourly Rate × 2,000 = Gross Annual Income<br>
      <span class="comment">// Example</span><br>
      <span class="highlight">$65/hr × 2,000 = $130,000 gross per year</span><br><br>
      <span class="comment">// But a W-2 contract worker also lacks benefits. Adjust:</span><br>
      $130,000 × 0.65 (after tax + benefit cost equiv.) ≈ <span class="highlight">$84,500 salary equivalent</span><br><br>
      <span class="comment">// The perm role at $90k also provides $20k in benefits (health, 401k, PTO)</span><br>
      <span class="comment">// Total perm package = ~$110k. So $65/hr on W-2 is actually LOWER in total.</span><br>
      <span class="comment">// That's why C2H rates are usually higher than perm salary equivalents.</span>
    </div>

