<!-- ─── PAGE: W2 / 1099 ─── -->
<div id="pg-w2_1099" class="page">
  <div class="hero">
    <div class="part-badge">Part A · Section 3</div>
    <h2>W-2, 1099 &amp; The Compliance Line You Must Never Cross</h2>
    <p>These are tax classifications, not just labels. Misclassifying a worker exposes your agency to federal penalties. Understand both types before you place a single candidate.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="content-wrap">

    <div class="tabs">
      <div class="tab active" onclick="switchTab(this,'t-w2')">W-2 Employee</div>
      <div class="tab" onclick="switchTab(this,'t-1099')">1099 Contractor</div>
      <div class="tab" onclick="switchTab(this,'t-compare')">Side-by-Side Comparison</div>
      <div class="tab" onclick="switchTab(this,'t-w2rule')">The W-2 Only Rule</div>
    </div>

    <div id="t-w2" class="tab-content active">
      <h2 class="sec-title">W-2 Employment — What It Means</h2>
      <p class="sec-sub">W-2 is the standard US employment tax classification. It covers both permanent hires AND contract workers placed through a staffing agency.</p>
      <div class="cards-grid">
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-badge">The Employer Does</div>
          <h3>Tax Withholding</h3>
          <p>Withholds income tax, Social Security (6.2%), and Medicare (1.45%) from every paycheck. Issues a W-2 form by January 31 showing total wages and taxes withheld.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-badge">The Candidate Gets</div>
          <h3>Simplified Tax Filing</h3>
          <p>Receives a paycheck with taxes already deducted. Files a simplified annual tax return. Shares FICA (Social Security + Medicare) costs 50/50 with the employer — each pays 7.65%.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-badge">In Staffing</div>
          <h3>Agency is Employer of Record</h3>
          <p>The AGENCY is the W-2 employer of record, not the client. The agency handles all payroll taxes, insurance, and compliance. The client pays the agency a bill rate. This is why your margin must cover more than overhead — it covers payroll tax matching.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="callout warn">
        <div class="callout-icon">⚠️</div>
        <div class="callout-body">
          <h4>FICA Tax Split — W-2 vs 1099</h4>
          <p>On a $100,000 income:<br>
          <strong>W-2 employee</strong> pays ~$7,650 in FICA (employer matches another $7,650)<br>
          <strong>1099 contractor</strong> pays ~$15,300 in self-employment tax (they are both employee AND employer)<br><br>
          This is why contractors charge higher hourly rates — they are compensating for the doubled tax burden and lack of benefits.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div id="t-1099" class="tab-content">
      <h2 class="sec-title">1099 Contractor — Self-Employed, Higher Rate, Higher Risk</h2>
      <p class="sec-sub">1099 means self-employed. The candidate runs their own business. The client pays them directly. No middleman, no taxes withheld.</p>
      <div class="cards-grid">
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-badge">Key Characteristic</div>
          <h3>Self-Employed Business Owner</h3>
          <p>The contractor is running their own business. They set their rate, control how the work is done, and bear all financial risk. They invoice the client and receive the full amount — then pay taxes themselves quarterly.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-badge">Tax Burden</div>
          <h3>Self-Employment Tax: 15.3%</h3>
          <p>The contractor pays 15.3% FICA (vs 7.65% for a W-2 employee). They are both the employer and employee for tax purposes. They must file quarterly estimated taxes or face penalties.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-badge">IRS Control Test</div>
          <h3>Are They Truly Independent?</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Controls WHEN, WHERE, and HOW work is done (not the client)</li>
            <li>Uses their own equipment and tools</li>
            <li>Can work for multiple clients simultaneously</li>
            <li>Project-based work, not integrated into core business</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-badge">Perks</div>
          <h3>Deductible Expenses</h3>
          <p>Contractors can deduct: home office, equipment, software, professional development, health insurance premiums, retirement contributions. This partially offsets the higher tax burden.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="callout danger">
        <div class="callout-icon">🚫</div>
        <div class="callout-body">
          <h4>Misclassification — The Risk You Must Never Create</h4>
          <p>If a company treats a worker as 1099 but the IRS determines they meet employee criteria → automatic reclassification as W-2.<br><br>
          <strong>Penalties:</strong> 10–30% of unpaid back taxes + interest + state labour violations. On a $100k placement, back taxes alone could be $15,000–$30,000 across potentially dozens of simultaneous placements.<br><br>
          <strong>As a recruiter:</strong> If a client says W-2 only and you submit a 1099 arrangement → your agency is liable. Never structure a placement as 1099 unless the client explicitly accepts it AND the worker genuinely meets contractor criteria.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div id="t-compare" class="tab-content">
      <h2 class="sec-title">Employment Type Comparison</h2>
      <p class="sec-sub">Your daily reference table. Know this before your first live screening call.</p>
      <table class="comp-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Attribute</th><th>Permanent W-2</th><th>Contract 1099</th><th>C2H (Contract Phase)</th><th>C2H (Perm Phase)</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Employer</strong></td><td>Client directly</td><td>Contractor (self)</td><td>Staffing agency</td><td>Client directly</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Tax Form</strong></td><td>W-2</td><td>1099-NEC</td><td>W-2 (agency)</td><td>W-2 (client)</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Taxes Withheld?</strong></td><td class="good">Yes — employer</td><td class="bad">No — self-pay</td><td class="good">Yes — agency</td><td class="good">Yes — client</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Benefits</strong></td><td class="good">Full (health, 401k, PTO)</td><td class="bad">None — self-funded</td><td class="warn">Minimal or none</td><td class="good">Full benefits</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Duration</strong></td><td>Indefinite</td><td>Project/defined</td><td>3–6 months</td><td>Indefinite</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Pay Rate</strong></td><td>Salary</td><td class="good">High hourly</td><td class="good">Higher hourly</td><td class="warn">Lower salary (offset by benefits)</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Job Security</strong></td><td class="good">High</td><td class="bad">Low (project-based)</td><td class="bad">Low (trial)</td><td class="good">High</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Legal Protection</strong></td><td class="good">Full employment law</td><td class="bad">Limited</td><td>Agency + client laws</td><td class="good">Full employment law</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div id="t-w2rule" class="tab-content">
      <h2 class="sec-title">The W-2 Only Rule — The Compliance Line</h2>
      <p class="sec-sub">When a job order says "W-2 only" — this is a compliance requirement, not a preference. Violating it exposes your agency to tax penalties.</p>

      <div class="callout danger">
        <div class="callout-icon">🚫</div>
        <div class="callout-body">
          <h4>What "W-2 Only" Means — Be Precise</h4>
          <p>The candidate must be employed as a W-2 employee — either of the staffing agency (for contract) or of the client (for permanent).<br><br>
          The client will <strong>NOT</strong> accept a 1099 independent contractor — even if the candidate is willing.<br>
          The client will <strong>NOT</strong> accept Corp-to-Corp (C2C) arrangements where a candidate bills through their own LLC.<br><br>
          This is standard for most enterprise clients, all healthcare clients, most government clients, and most Fortune 500 companies.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3 style="font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;font-size:20px;color:var(--navy);margin:24px 0 14px;">Which Work Auth Types Satisfy "W-2 Only"?</h3>
      <table class="comp-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Work Auth Type</th><th>W-2 Compatible?</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>US Citizen (USC)</td><td class="good">✓ Yes</td><td>Always W-2 eligible. Cleanest submission.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Green Card / Permanent Resident</td><td class="good">✓ Yes</td><td>Always W-2 eligible. No expiry tracking needed.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>TN Visa holder</td><td class="good">✓ Yes</td><td>W-2 through agency. New TN needed if changing employer.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>EAD (OPT / H-4 EAD)</td><td class="warn">⚠ Verify expiry</td><td>W-2 compatible but check expiry date covers the full contract.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>H-1B</td><td class="warn">⚠ Transfer needed</td><td>W-2 only, but most enterprise clients also exclude H-1B transfer. Check the req.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>1099 Independent Contractor</td><td class="bad">✗ No</td><td>Compliance violation on a W-2 only req.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Corp-to-Corp (C2C)</td><td class="bad">✗ No</td><td>Compliance violation. Never promise C2C on W-2 only.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <div class="callout warn">
        <div class="callout-icon">📞</div>
        <div class="callout-body">
          <h4>On the Screening Call — What to Do</h4>
          <p>
          <strong>Always ask first:</strong> "Can you confirm your work authorisation status?"<br><br>
          <strong>If 1099 or C2C:</strong> "This position is W-2 only — the client does not accept 1099 or C2C. Are you willing to be on W-2?" If no → end the call professionally.<br><br>
          <strong>If H-1B:</strong> Check the job order immediately. If it says "No H-1B transfer" → end the call professionally. If H-1B is allowed → flag to manager before proceeding. H-1B transfer adds weeks.<br><br>
          <strong>Never promise</strong> a candidate they can work 1099 on a W-2 only requirement — the MSP will reject the profile and your agency bears the liability.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

