<div id="pg-visa_overview" class="page">
  <div class="hero">
    <div class="part-badge">Part B · Section 1</div>
    <h2>US Visa Overview — Every Type You Will Encounter on a Call</h2>
    <p>All US visas split into two categories: immigrant (permanent) and non-immigrant (temporary). The category determines whether work authorisation is permanent or restricted.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="content-wrap">

    <div class="cards-grid">
      <div class="card c2h">
        <div class="card-badge">Immigrant Visa</div>
        <h3>Green Card Path — Permanent</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Intent: permanent residence and eventual US citizenship</li>
          <li>Work auth: unlimited — any employer, any role</li>
          <li>Duration: permanent (Green Card renewed every 10 years)</li>
          <li>Result: Lawful Permanent Resident (LPR)</li>
          <li>Path to citizenship: 3 years (married to USC) or 5 years (other paths)</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card contract">
        <div class="card-badge">Non-Immigrant Visa</div>
        <h3>Temporary Stay — Restricted</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Intent: temporary presence for specific purpose (work, study, business)</li>
          <li>Work auth: restricted — tied to employer, purpose, or duration</li>
          <li>Duration: varies by type (3 months to 6 years typically)</li>
          <li>Result: worker must leave, extend, or change status when visa expires</li>
          <li>Examples: F-1 (student), H-1B (worker), L-1 (intra-company), TN (NAFTA)</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout gold">
      <div class="callout-icon">⭐</div>
      <div class="callout-body">
        <h4>The Recruiter's Work Auth Hierarchy</h4>
        <p><strong>Green Card = EAD &gt; USC &gt; TN &gt; H-1B</strong><br><br>
        Green Card and USC are always the cleanest submissions — no expiry tracking, no employer dependency, no transfer delays. When you find a Green Card candidate, submit fast — every other agency is looking for them too. EAD is good if the expiry date covers the contract period. H-1B is the most complex — employer-tied, transfer required, cap lottery risk.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <hr class="divider">

    <div class="tabs">
      <div class="tab active" onclick="switchTab(this,'vt-f1')">F-1 / OPT / STEM OPT</div>
      <div class="tab" onclick="switchTab(this,'vt-h1b')">H-1B</div>
      <div class="tab" onclick="switchTab(this,'vt-gc')">Green Card & EAD</div>
      <div class="tab" onclick="switchTab(this,'vt-other')">TN / L-1 / O-1</div>
    </div>

    <!-- F1 TAB -->
    <div id="vt-f1" class="tab-content active">
      <h2 class="sec-title">F-1 Student Visa, OPT & STEM OPT</h2>
      <p class="sec-sub">Millions of international students graduate from US universities every year. Their work auth follows a specific path. Know each stage — this is one of the most common work auth types you will encounter in IT and tech recruiting.</p>

      <div class="flow-row">
        <div class="flow-step">
          <div class="step-num">Stage 1</div>
          <h4>F-1 Active (In School)</h4>
          <p>Full-time student status. On-campus work only (≤20 hrs/week during semester). Cannot work off-campus without OPT or CPT. CPT: 12-month integrated work during curriculum.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
        <div class="flow-step">
          <div class="step-num">Stage 2</div>
          <h4>OPT (12 months)</h4>
          <p>Post-graduation work auth. Valid for ANY employer — not tied to one company. Must use within 14 months of graduation. Does NOT require employer sponsorship.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
        <div class="flow-step">
          <div class="step-num">Stage 3</div>
          <h4>STEM OPT (+24 months)</h4>
          <p>STEM field graduates only (CS, Engineering, Math, Science). Additional 24 months = 36 months total. Employer must be E-Verify enrolled. Must have Form I-983 training plan.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
        <div class="flow-step">
          <div class="step-num">Stage 4</div>
          <h4>H-1B (if sponsored)</h4>
          <p>Employer files H-1B petition. Annual lottery cap: 85,000 visas. If approved: 3+3 years. If denied or no sponsor: must depart US.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3 style="font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;font-size:20px;color:var(--navy);margin:28px 0 14px;">Your OPT/STEM OPT Recruiter Checklist</h3>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><input type="checkbox"><div class="cl-text">Ask: <strong>"What type of work auth do you have?"</strong> → if OPT, ask: "Is it standard OPT or STEM OPT?"</div></li>
        <li><input type="checkbox"><div class="cl-text">Ask: <strong>"What is your EAD expiry date?"</strong> — this is the hard deadline for the placement</div></li>
        <li><input type="checkbox"><div class="cl-text">Check: <strong>Will the contract end BEFORE the EAD expires?</strong> If contract = 6 months and OPT expires in 4 months → DO NOT SUBMIT without flagging</div></li>
        <li><input type="checkbox"><div class="cl-text">Ask: <strong>"Have you applied for H-1B sponsorship?"</strong> → if yes, what is the status? Pending lottery may create a gap.</div></li>
        <li><input type="checkbox"><div class="cl-text">Understand <strong>Cap-Gap:</strong> OPT expires April 1 + H-1B filed + receipt notice = automatic extension until October 1 (H-1B start date). Candidate can continue working during cap-gap ONLY with I-20 extension and valid I-797C receipt notice.</div></li>
      </ul>

      <h3 style="font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;font-size:20px;color:var(--navy);margin:28px 0 14px;">OPT/STEM OPT Placement Risks</h3>
      <div class="cards-grid">
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-badge" style="background:#fde8e8;color:#c0392b;">Risk 1</div>
          <h3>EAD Expires Mid-Contract</h3>
          <p>If OPT expires before contract ends, the candidate must stop working. The MSP will immediately remove them. Always verify expiry covers the full contract period — and add a buffer month.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-badge" style="background:#fde8e8;color:#c0392b;">Risk 2</div>
          <h3>No STEM Extension</h3>
          <p>Not all STEM fields qualify. The employer must be E-Verify enrolled for STEM OPT. If the employer isn't enrolled, the STEM extension is invalid. Verify E-Verify status before placing.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <div class="card-badge" style="background:#fde8e8;color:#c0392b;">Risk 3</div>
          <h3>H-1B Lottery Denial</h3>
          <p>If the candidate is in the lottery and doesn't win, they must depart after OPT expires. You may lose a placed candidate mid-contract. Flag any OPT candidate with an active lottery petition to your manager.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- H1B TAB -->
    <div id="vt-h1b" class="tab-content">
      <h2 class="sec-title">H-1B Specialty Occupation Visa</h2>
      <p class="sec-sub">H-1B is the most common work visa for skilled professionals. It is also the most restrictive and the most often client-rejected visa type you will encounter.</p>
      <div class="visa-card h1b" style="margin:0 0 20px;">
        <div class="vc-header">
          <div><h3>H-1B Visa</h3><p style="font-size:12px;color:var(--text-light);margin-top:3px;">Specialty Occupation — Employer-Tied</p></div>
          <div class="vc-code">H1B</div>
        </div>
        <div class="vc-row"><span class="vc-label">Purpose</span><span class="vc-val">Temporary employment for "specialty occupation" workers — roles requiring at minimum a bachelor's degree. Common in IT, engineering, accounting, finance, medicine.</span></div>
        <div class="vc-row"><span class="vc-label">Duration</span><span class="vc-val">Initial 3 years. Extension to 6 years maximum. Further extensions if green card process is in progress.</span></div>
        <div class="vc-row"><span class="vc-label">Annual Cap</span><span class="vc-val">85,000 visas/year (65,000 regular + 20,000 US master's holders). If demand exceeds cap → lottery. Recent odds: ~1 in 3.</span></div>
        <div class="vc-row"><span class="vc-label">Employer-Tied</span><span class="vc-val">Tied to the SPECIFIC EMPLOYER who sponsored it. Candidate cannot simply change jobs — must get H-1B portability transfer via AC21.</span></div>
        <div class="vc-row"><span class="vc-label">Prevailing Wage</span><span class="vc-val">Employer MUST pay the H-1B worker the prevailing wage for the role and location (set by DOL). Cannot underpay to save costs.</span></div>
        <div class="vc-row"><span class="vc-label">Cost to Sponsor</span><span class="vc-val">USCIS filing ($460) + legal fees ($1,500–$3,000) + optional premium processing ($2,500) = $4,000–$7,000+ total</span></div>
        <div class="vc-tip">Most staffing agencies do NOT sponsor new H-1B visas — the cost, lottery risk, and timeline make it impractical for contract placements. If a candidate says "I need H-1B sponsorship" → your agency almost certainly cannot help. Be empathetic but clear: "We don't sponsor H-1B visas — you'd need to find a direct employer. If you obtain your own H-1B later, we'd love to work with you."</div>
      </div>

      <h3 style="font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;font-size:20px;color:var(--navy);margin:20px 0 14px;">H-1B Candidate Decision Flow on a Call</h3>
      <div class="flow-row">
        <div class="flow-step"><div class="step-num">Step 1</div><h4>Candidate says: "I am on H-1B"</h4><p>Don't proceed yet. Open the job order first.</p></div>
        <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
        <div class="flow-step"><div class="step-num">Step 2</div><h4>Check job order</h4><p>"Does this req say No H-1B?"</p></div>
        <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
        <div class="flow-step"><div class="step-num">Step 3a</div><h4>If YES: No H-1B</h4><p>End call politely. Do not proceed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="flow-row" style="margin-top:12px;">
        <div class="flow-step"><div class="step-num">Step 3b</div><h4>If H-1B Allowed</h4><p>Ask: "Who is your current H-1B sponsor?" "Are you open to transfer?"</p></div>
        <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
        <div class="flow-step"><div class="step-num">Step 4</div><h4>Ask: I-94 Expiry</h4><p>"What is the expiry date on your H-1B approval / I-94?"</p></div>
        <div class="flow-arrow">→</div>
        <div class="flow-step"><div class="step-num">Step 5</div><h4>Flag to Manager</h4><p>H-1B transfer adds weeks. Always loop in manager before proceeding.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- GC TAB -->
    <div id="vt-gc" class="tab-content">
      <h2 class="sec-title">Green Card & EAD — The Best Work Auth Outcomes</h2>
      <p class="sec-sub">Green Card holders have unlimited work authorisation. EAD holders have temporary but flexible work authorisation. Know the difference intimately — these two categories dominate your daily submissions.</p>

      <div class="visa-grid">
        <div class="visa-card gc">
          <div class="vc-header">
            <div><h3>Green Card</h3><p style="font-size:12px;color:var(--text-light);margin-top:3px;">Permanent Resident Card — Form I-551</p></div>
            <div class="vc-code">GC</div>
          </div>
          <div class="vc-row"><span class="vc-label">Status</span><span class="vc-val">Lawful Permanent Resident (LPR). Not a visa — a permanent immigration status.</span></div>
          <div class="vc-row"><span class="vc-label">Work Auth</span><span class="vc-val">Unlimited. Any employer, any role, any time. No sponsorship, no expiry, no restrictions.</span></div>
          <div class="vc-row"><span class="vc-label">Physical Card</span><span class="vc-val">Credit-card size. Predominantly green with Statue of Liberty holographic image. Expires every 10 years (for renewal — status itself is permanent).</span></div>
          <div class="vc-row"><span class="vc-label">I-9 Verification</span><span class="vc-val">List A document — satisfies both identity AND work authorisation. No additional documents needed.</span></div>
          <div class="vc-row"><span class="vc-label">Pathways</span><span class="vc-val">Employment-based (EB-1 to EB-5) · Family-sponsored · Refugee/Asylee · Diversity Visa Lottery</span></div>
          <div class="vc-tip">🌟 Green Card = the recruiter's dream. Prioritise GC candidates for submission. No expiry tracking, no employer dependency, no transfer delays. When you find a Green Card candidate who matches the req, submit first — every other agency is also looking for them.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="visa-card ead">
          <div class="vc-header">
            <div><h3>EAD</h3><p style="font-size:12px;color:var(--text-light);margin-top:3px;">Employment Authorization Document — Form I-766</p></div>
            <div class="vc-code">EAD</div>
          </div>
          <div class="vc-row"><span class="vc-label">Status</span><span class="vc-val">Temporary work permit issued to various immigration categories — OPT graduates, H-4 dependents, pending GC applicants, asylees, DACA recipients.</span></div>
          <div class="vc-row"><span class="vc-label">Work Auth</span><span class="vc-val">Depends on category. Most EADs allow any employer. H-4 EAD is restricted. Always check the visa category printed on the card.</span></div>
          <div class="vc-row"><span class="vc-label">Physical Card</span><span class="vc-val">Credit-card size. Predominantly red (2017+ design) with bald eagle holographic image. Expiry date is CRITICAL — employment stops when EAD expires.</span></div>
          <div class="vc-row"><span class="vc-label">Key Question</span><span class="vc-val">Always ask: "What is your EAD expiry date?" and "What category is your EAD?" The category tells you the underlying immigration status.</span></div>
          <div class="vc-tip">⚠️ GC-EAD is a special case: some candidates have an EAD based on a pending green card application (Form I-485). These candidates may also hold an active H-1B simultaneously — they use the EAD for employer flexibility and keep H-1B as backup. Strong work auth position from a recruiter's perspective.</div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3 style="font-family:'Playfair Display',serif;font-size:20px;color:var(--navy);margin:24px 0 14px;">GC vs EAD — Quick Comparison</h3>
      <table class="comp-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Attribute</th><th>Green Card (I-551)</th><th>EAD (I-766)</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><strong>Work Auth</strong></td><td class="good">Unlimited, any employer</td><td class="warn">Depends on category — usually any, but confirm</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Expiry</strong></td><td class="good">10 yrs (for renewal — status permanent)</td><td class="bad">1–2 years — employment STOPS at expiry</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Employer Change</strong></td><td class="good">Freely — no notification required</td><td class="warn">Usually free — some categories restricted</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>International Travel</strong></td><td class="good">Yes — with valid GC</td><td class="warn">Varies — some need Advance Parole</td></tr>
          <tr><td><strong>Path to Citizenship</strong></td><td class="good">Yes — after 3–5 years</td><td class="warn">Depends on underlying status</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <!-- TN L1 O1 TAB -->
    <div id="vt-other" class="tab-content">
      <h2 class="sec-title">TN, L-1 & O-1 — Other Visa Types</h2>
      <p class="sec-sub">Less common than H-1B or Green Card — but you will see these on resumes. Know what each means and how to handle them on a call.</p>

      <div class="visa-grid">
        <div class="visa-card tn">
          <div class="vc-header">
            <div><h3>TN Visa</h3><p style="font-size:12px;color:var(--text-light);margin-top:3px;">NAFTA/USMCA Professional</p></div>
            <div class="vc-code">TN</div>
          </div>
          <div class="vc-row"><span class="vc-label">Who</span><span class="vc-val">Canadian and Mexican citizens ONLY — under USMCA (formerly NAFTA)</span></div>
          <div class="vc-row"><span class="vc-label">Occupations</span><span class="vc-val">Specific professional roles: engineers, accountants, scientists, computer systems analysts, lawyers, nurses, pharmacists</span></div>
          <div class="vc-row"><span class="vc-label">Duration</span><span class="vc-val">3 years, renewable indefinitely in 3-year increments — no maximum</span></div>
          <div class="vc-row"><span class="vc-label">Advantage</span><span class="vc-val">No lottery, no annual cap. Canadian citizens can self-petition at the border. Very fast processing.</span></div>
          <div class="vc-row"><span class="vc-label">Employer-Tied</span><span class="vc-val">Yes — tied to specific employer and job title. Changing employers requires a new TN.</span></div>
          <div class="vc-tip">TN is the most underrated option in US staffing. Canadian citizens can get a TN at the border in ONE DAY — no petition, no lottery, no waiting. For urgent reqs where H-1B transfers take too long, a TN-eligible Canadian can start almost immediately.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="visa-card l1">
          <div class="vc-header">
            <div><h3>L-1 Visa</h3><p style="font-size:12px;color:var(--text-light);margin-top:3px;">Intracompany Transfer</p></div>
            <div class="vc-code">L1</div>
          </div>
          <div class="vc-row"><span class="vc-label">Who</span><span class="vc-val">Managers/executives (L-1A) or specialised knowledge workers (L-1B) transferred within the same multinational company</span></div>
          <div class="vc-row"><span class="vc-label">Requirement</span><span class="vc-val">Employee must have worked for the company 1+ year in the past 3 years at a foreign office</span></div>
          <div class="vc-row"><span class="vc-label">Duration</span><span class="vc-val">L-1A: up to 7 years. L-1B: up to 5 years</span></div>
          <div class="vc-row"><span class="vc-label">GC Path</span><span class="vc-val">L-1A holders can pursue EB-1C (multinational manager) green card — faster track than EB-2/EB-3</span></div>
          <div class="vc-row"><span class="vc-label">Tied To</span><span class="vc-val">Specific company ONLY — cannot change employers on L-1</span></div>
          <div class="vc-tip">L-1 candidates typically work for large multinationals — IBM, Infosys, Tata, Wipro, Cognizant. If they say L-1 and are looking at your contract, ask who their current L-1 sponsor is. The L-1 needs to be transferred or they need to move to a different visa.</div>
        </div>

        <div class="visa-card o1">
          <div class="vc-header">
            <div><h3>O-1 Visa</h3><p style="font-size:12px;color:var(--text-light);margin-top:3px;">Extraordinary Ability</p></div>
            <div class="vc-code">O1</div>
          </div>
          <div class="vc-row"><span class="vc-label">Who</span><span class="vc-val">Individuals with extraordinary ability in sciences, arts, business, athletics, or media — demonstrated through awards, publications, peer recognition</span></div>
          <div class="vc-row"><span class="vc-label">Duration</span><span class="vc-val">3 years initial + 1-year extensions (no maximum)</span></div>
          <div class="vc-row"><span class="vc-label">Advantage</span><span class="vc-val">No annual cap, no lottery. Can self-petition in some cases.</span></div>
          <div class="vc-row"><span class="vc-label">Work Auth</span><span class="vc-val">Tied to specific employer/agent who files the petition</span></div>
          <div class="vc-tip">O-1 holders are typically senior and specialised (researchers, executives, specialists). If you see this on a resume, it signals a high-value candidate. Work auth situation is similar to H-1B — employer-tied, transfer needed. Flag to manager.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

