<!-- ─── PAGE: MISTAKES ─── -->
<div id="pg-mistakes" class="page">
  <div class="hero">
    <div class="part-badge">Reference</div>
    <h2>8 Common Recruiter Mistakes — Employment Types &amp; Work Auth</h2>
    <p>Every one of these has happened. Recently. Some are compliance violations. Know them before you make them — not after.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="content-wrap">
    <div class="mistakes-grid">
      <div class="mistake">
        <div class="m-num">1</div>
        <h4>Submitting a 1099 candidate to a W-2 only req</h4>
        <p>Compliance violation. The MSP will reject the profile and your agency risks misclassification liability. Always check the req employment type before beginning the screening call — not after.</p>
        <div class="fix">Check the job order's employment type requirement before your first word on the call.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="mistake">
        <div class="m-num">2</div>
        <h4>Not asking for EAD expiry date</h4>
        <p>Submitting an OPT candidate whose EAD expires in 2 months for a 6-month contract. They get placed, the EAD expires, they stop work — MSP removes them and your agency looks incompetent. This is also a compliance issue.</p>
        <div class="fix">Always ask "What is your EAD expiry date?" as a mandatory follow-up to any OPT/EAD response.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="mistake">
        <div class="m-num">3</div>
        <h4>Assuming H-1B is acceptable because the req says "W-2 only"</h4>
        <p>A req says "W-2 only" and the fresher submits an H-1B candidate thinking W-2 + H-1B is compatible. It IS W-2 compatible — but most enterprise clients ALSO say "no H-1B transfer" separately. Read the full req, not just the employment type field.</p>
        <div class="fix">Read the entire job order — employment type AND work auth restrictions are separate fields.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="mistake">
        <div class="m-num">4</div>
        <h4>Promising H-1B sponsorship</h4>
        <p>Candidate asks if your agency will sponsor their H-1B. Fresher says "I'll check" and forgets to follow up — or worse, implies yes. Your agency almost certainly does NOT sponsor new H-1Bs. Be clear upfront and honest.</p>
        <div class="fix">Default response: "Our agency doesn't sponsor H-1B visas — you'd need a direct employer for that."</div>
      </div>
      <div class="mistake">
        <div class="m-num">5</div>
        <h4>Accepting "Green Card" at face value without checking expiry</h4>
        <p>Candidate says "I have a Green Card" but the physical card expired 2 years ago. An expired Green Card IS still valid residency status (it auto-renews while renewal is pending) — but always verify the card's physical expiry for I-9 purposes.</p>
        <div class="fix">Ask: "Is your Green Card card current?" — status is permanent, but the physical card must be valid for I-9.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="mistake">
        <div class="m-num">6</div>
        <h4>Not distinguishing OPT from STEM OPT</h4>
        <p>Standard OPT = 12 months. STEM OPT = 36 months total. If you assume standard OPT when it's STEM OPT, you undercount available work months — potentially rejecting a candidate who had plenty of time left on their work auth.</p>
        <div class="fix">Always ask: "Is it standard OPT or STEM OPT?" — two different timelines, two different requirements.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="mistake">
        <div class="m-num">7</div>
        <h4>Ignoring the "GC/USC only" requirement for healthcare</h4>
        <p>Some healthcare and government clients specify Green Card or US Citizen ONLY — not just W-2. An OPT or EAD candidate on W-2 still doesn't meet this requirement. W-2 eligible ≠ GC/USC only eligible. These are separate criteria.</p>
        <div class="fix">Healthcare reqs: check both (a) W-2 only AND (b) GC/USC only as separate requirements.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="mistake">
        <div class="m-num">8</div>
        <h4>Asking work auth at the wrong stage of the call</h4>
        <p>Asking "What is your work authorisation?" after spending 20 minutes on the skill matrix and negotiating pay — only to discover they need H-1B sponsorship. That entire conversation was wasted. Work auth comes BEFORE the full qualification pitch.</p>
        <div class="fix">Sequence: (1) Confirm identity → (2) Brief pitch/interest check → (3) Work auth question → (4) Proceed only if compatible.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout gold" style="margin-top:28px;">
      <div class="callout-icon">📌</div>
      <div class="callout-body">
        <h4>The Universal Pre-Call Checklist</h4>
        <p>Before every screening call, spend 30 seconds checking these on the job order:<br>
        <strong>1.</strong> What is the employment type? (W-2 / 1099 / C2C accepted?)<br>
        <strong>2.</strong> Any specific work auth restrictions? (No H-1B? GC/USC only?)<br>
        <strong>3.</strong> Is this a contract, perm, or C2H role?<br><br>
        Then on the call, ask work auth BEFORE you pitch the full role. 30 seconds of prep saves 20 minutes of wasted screening.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

