<div id="pg-glossary" class="page">
  <div class="hero">
    <div class="part-badge">Reference</div>
    <h2>Glossary — Every Term You Need to Know</h2>
    <p>A practitioner-level reference. Every term here will appear in your daily work — job orders, candidate conversations, ATS notes, and client communications.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="content-wrap">
    <h2 class="sec-title">Employment Types & Tax</h2>
    <div class="gloss-grid">
      <div class="gloss-item"><div class="term">W-2</div><div class="def">US tax form + employment classification. The employer withholds income tax, Social Security (6.2%), and Medicare (1.45%) from each paycheck. In staffing, the agency is the W-2 employer of record.</div></div>
      <div class="gloss-item"><div class="term">1099 / 1099-NEC</div><div class="def">Tax form for self-employed contractors. The client pays the full invoice; the contractor pays their own quarterly taxes (15.3% self-employment tax). No employer withholding.</div></div>
      <div class="gloss-item"><div class="term">C2C (Corp-to-Corp)</div><div class="def">The candidate bills through their own LLC or S-Corp instead of directly as an individual. The agency receives an invoice from the candidate's company. Rejected on W-2 only requirements.</div></div>
      <div class="gloss-item"><div class="term">W-2 Only</div><div class="def">Client requirement meaning the candidate MUST be employed as a W-2 employee. Excludes 1099 independent contractors and C2C arrangements. Most enterprise, healthcare, and government clients require this.</div></div>
      <div class="gloss-item"><div class="term">PERM / Direct Hire</div><div class="def">The candidate is hired directly by the client company as a permanent, full-time employee. No defined end date. Agency earns a one-time placement fee (15–25% of first-year salary).</div></div>
      <div class="gloss-item"><div class="term">C2H (Contract-to-Hire)</div><div class="def">Starts as a contract (3–6 months) with a built-in path to permanent employment. During the contract phase, the agency is the employer of record. Upon conversion, the client takes over as the direct employer.</div></div>
      <div class="gloss-item"><div class="term">FICA</div><div class="def">Federal Insurance Contributions Act. Covers Social Security (12.4% split 50/50 between employer/employee) and Medicare (2.9% split 50/50). A 1099 contractor pays the full 15.3% themselves.</div></div>
      <div class="gloss-item"><div class="term">Bill Rate</div><div class="def">The hourly rate the client pays the staffing agency. Includes the candidate's pay rate plus the agency's margin (which covers payroll taxes, overhead, insurance, and profit).</div></div>
      <div class="gloss-item"><div class="term">Employer of Record</div><div class="def">The legal entity responsible for employment taxes, W-2 issuance, payroll, workers' compensation, and compliance. In staffing, this is the agency — not the client where the candidate is physically working.</div></div>
      <div class="gloss-item"><div class="term">At-Will Employment</div><div class="def">The default US employment doctrine: either the employer or employee can terminate the relationship at any time, for any reason (with some legal exceptions). Makes contract hiring less risky for employers.</div></div>
    </div>

    <h2 class="sec-title" style="margin-top:36px;">Visa Types & Work Auth</h2>
    <div class="gloss-grid">
      <div class="gloss-item"><div class="term">USC (US Citizen)</div><div class="def">Full and unconditional US citizenship. Unlimited work authorisation — any employer, any role, no restrictions or expiry. Born in the US, naturalised, or born abroad to US citizens.</div></div>
      <div class="gloss-item"><div class="term">Green Card (I-551)</div><div class="def">Lawful Permanent Resident card. Not a visa — a permanent immigration status. Unlimited work authorisation. Valid for 10 years (for renewal); the underlying status is permanent.</div></div>
      <div class="gloss-item"><div class="term">F-1 Visa</div><div class="def">US student visa for full-time academic study. Does not authorise off-campus employment unless the student has OPT or CPT. The most common visa category for international students.</div></div>
      <div class="gloss-item"><div class="term">OPT (Optional Practical Training)</div><div class="def">Post-graduation work authorisation for F-1 students. 12 months for standard graduates. Must be used within 14 months of graduation. Valid for any employer — not tied to one company. Documented by an EAD card.</div></div>
      <div class="gloss-item"><div class="term">STEM OPT</div><div class="def">24-month extension of OPT for STEM field graduates (CS, Engineering, Math, Sciences). Total = 36 months. Employer must be E-Verify enrolled. Requires Form I-983 training plan.</div></div>
      <div class="gloss-item"><div class="term">EAD (I-766)</div><div class="def">Employment Authorization Document. Physical card (red, 2017+ design) issued to various immigration categories — OPT graduates, H-4 dependents, pending GC applicants, asylees, DACA recipients. Check expiry date and category.</div></div>
      <div class="gloss-item"><div class="term">H-1B</div><div class="def">Specialty occupation visa for workers in roles requiring a bachelor's degree. Employer-tied. 3+3 year duration. Annual cap of 85,000 with lottery. Transfer required to change employers. Most restrictive common visa type.</div></div>
      <div class="gloss-item"><div class="term">H-4 EAD</div><div class="def">Work authorisation issued to H-1B spouses (H-4 visa holders) whose H-1B spouse is in the green card process. Allows the spouse to work for any employer. Check expiry and confirm category on the card.</div></div>
      <div class="gloss-item"><div class="term">TN Visa</div><div class="def">USMCA/NAFTA professional visa for Canadian and Mexican citizens only. Specific eligible professions. No lottery, no annual cap. Renewable in 3-year increments with no maximum. Employer-tied — new TN needed if changing jobs.</div></div>
      <div class="gloss-item"><div class="term">L-1 Visa</div><div class="def">Intracompany transfer visa for managers/executives (L-1A) or specialised knowledge workers (L-1B). Must have worked at a foreign office of the same company for 1+ year. L-1A path to EB-1C green card is faster than EB-2/EB-3.</div></div>
      <div class="gloss-item"><div class="term">O-1 Visa</div><div class="def">Extraordinary ability visa for individuals with exceptional skill in their field (sciences, arts, business, athletics, media). No annual cap, no lottery. Employer-tied. Rare — signals a high-value senior candidate.</div></div>
      <div class="gloss-item"><div class="term">LPR (Lawful Permanent Resident)</div><div class="def">The official immigration status of a Green Card holder. Can live and work permanently in the US. Can apply for US citizenship after 3–5 years. Referred to interchangeably with "Green Card holder."</div></div>
      <div class="gloss-item"><div class="term">Cap-Gap</div><div class="def">Automatic extension of OPT work authorisation between OPT expiry (April 1) and H-1B start date (October 1), if a timely H-1B petition was filed and receipt notice (I-797C) is in hand. Candidate can continue working during cap-gap.</div></div>
      <div class="gloss-item"><div class="term">H-1B Transfer (Portability)</div><div class="def">The process of moving an existing H-1B from one employer to another via AC21 portability. The candidate can start work when the new employer's petition is received (I-797C receipt). Takes 3–8 weeks. Not all clients will wait.</div></div>
      <div class="gloss-item"><div class="term">I-9 Verification</div><div class="def">Federal employer requirement to verify employee identity and work authorisation. Must be completed within 3 business days of hire. Green Card and EAD are both "List A" documents — satisfy both identity and work auth.</div></div>
      <div class="gloss-item"><div class="term">E-Verify</div><div class="def">Federal online database that employers use to verify the work authorisation of new hires by checking against DHS and SSA records. Enrollment is voluntary for most private employers — but REQUIRED for STEM OPT placements.</div></div>
      <div class="gloss-item"><div class="term">Prevailing Wage</div><div class="def">The minimum wage an employer must pay an H-1B worker for a specific role and location, as determined by the Department of Labor (DOL). Employers cannot pay H-1B workers below prevailing wage to save costs.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout gold" style="margin-top:28px;">
      <div class="callout-icon">📚</div>
      <div class="callout-body">
        <h4>Session Recap — 8 Things That Must Be Automatic</h4>
        <p>
        <strong>01</strong> W-2 = employer withholds taxes, agency is employer of record in staffing.<br>
        <strong>02</strong> 1099 = self-employed, self-taxed, 15.3% FICA, higher rate, higher risk.<br>
        <strong>03</strong> C2H = contract first, permanent if it works — "paid interview" pitch.<br>
        <strong>04</strong> W-2 only = compliance line, not preference — never submit 1099 or C2C.<br>
        <strong>05</strong> Green Card = unlimited work auth, cleanest submission, prioritise for submission.<br>
        <strong>06</strong> OPT = check the EAD expiry date against the contract end date first.<br>
        <strong>07</strong> H-1B = employer-tied and often client-rejected — check job order before spending time on a call.<br>
        <strong>08</strong> Work auth question comes AFTER brief pitch and interest check, BEFORE full qualification.
        </p>
      </div>
